\section*{Milnor K reconstruction at the characteristic prime}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 009; 2026-10-05), the asserted result is that Milnor $K$-theory modulo the characteristic recovers such function fields from degree-two Steinberg relations, up to one nonzero scalar.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Fix characteristic $p>0$. For any field $F$ of that characteristic put
\[
 V_F=F^{\times}/(F^{\times})^p,\qquad
 R_F=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb F_p}\{[f]\otimes[1-f]:f\ne0,1\}.
\]
A linear isomorphism $\Theta:V_K\to V_L$ is compatible if $(\Theta\otimes\Theta)R_K=R_L$, equivalently preserving degree-two Milnor products. For finitely generated $K/k,L/l$ with algebraically closed constants of characteristic $p$ and both relative transcendence degrees at least two, the asserted reconstruction bijection is
\[
 \operatorname{Isom}(K,k;L,l)\xrightarrow{\sim}
 \operatorname{Isom}_{\rm M}(V_K,V_L)/\mathbb F_p^{\times}.
\]
On the left, field isomorphisms carry $k$ onto $l$; on the right compatible maps are identified under nonzero scalar multiplication. Only the first two Milnor groups modulo the characteristic are used.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 009; 2026-10-05), vanishing symbols are interpreted through derivations over the subfield of $p$th powers. An intersection calculation recovers projective lines; one common power correction aligns these lines, and projective incidence gives the field operations.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The source requires invertible $\mathbb F_p$-linear maps and equality $(\Theta\otimes\Theta)(R_K)=R_L$, rather than arbitrary maps preserving only some relations. With those conventions, its theorem gives the entire reconstruction bijection in this question for relative transcendence degree at least two. The field maps carry the named constant field onto the target constant field; the common nonzero scalar quotient appears on the Milnor side.

\subsection{Formal verification}
